# Road-Preview Sensing for Active Vehicle Suspension:
# Performance Benefits, Control Strategies, and Implementation Challenges

## Abstract

To be viable, modern vehicle suspension systems must effectively balance ride comfort, stability and safety under all road conditions. Active suspension systems are certainly a step up from passive designs, effectively utilizing sensors, electronic controllers, and actuators to enhance ride smoothness. Most systems on the road today are reliant on reactive feedback control. These systems only alter the suspension after one of the wheels faces an irregularity on the road, which makes some minor bumpiness inevitable. Road-preview suspension systems overcome this restriction by instead estimating the upcoming road profile with cameras, LiDAR, inertial measurement units, and sensor-­fusion algorithms. This preview information can be combined with feedforward and Model Predictive Control techniques to initiate suspension actions prior to wheel impact. 
This paper discusses how much road-­preview sensing benefits active suspension performance over conventional feedback-­only control. Literature studying road-­elevation identification, three-­dimensional road-­profile estimation, preview-­based suspension control, and predictive control architectures are reviewed. Results found in the literature show that preview information allows for decreases in vehicle-­body acceleration, better disturbance rejection, and improvements to ride comfort and stability by allowing the controller to "see" road irregularities before they impact the vehicle. However, accurate road-­profile estimation, low-­latency computation, reliable sensing in varying environmental conditions, and adequate actuator capacity are all necessary for the road-­preview approach to succeed. Implementing these sensing systems also comes at the cost of increased price, computation, calibration needs, and overall complexity. Overall, road-­preview sensing is a substantial development in automotive mechatronics that evolves active suspension control from a reactive to predictive sense-­predict-act architecture. This development may be crucial moving forward as electric, connected, and autonomous vehicles increase the need for adaptable chassis systems and passenger comfort.

## I. Introduction

Suspension systems are tuned to meet ride comfort, handling stability, and passenger safety requirements. Suspension systems isolate the vehicle body from road disturbances while maintaining tire contact with the road [1], [2]. Consequently, this creates several design objectives that can conflict with each other. Suspension tuning seeks to compromise these objectives in the most ideal way possible. Too soft of a suspension can lead to passenger discomfort from poor handling stability. Too stiff of a suspension can maximize road holding but transmit more vibration into the vehicle body [1], [2]. Balancing these objectives becomes extremely challenging when the vehicle operates over varying road surfaces, speeds, and driving conditions [1].
Conventional passive suspensions have a mechanical characteristic that is given and typically involves passive elements, such as springs and dampers. Passive suspensions are simple, reliable, and inexpensive, but offer little flexibility in terms of dynamic performance [1]. By contrast, semi-active and active suspensions typically involve some form of feedback control and actively controlled or powered suspension elements for improved performance. Active suspension systems can produce normal forces affecting vehicle-body motion [1], [2].
The literature reports a variety of approaches related to the control of active suspensions. However, most solutions rely on the concept of feedback control [1], [2]. Consequently, control forces are generated following a road disturbance and after its effects have been identified by the relevant sensors. A feedback controller can effectively reduce a disturbance, but it cannot prevent it. The response of a feedback-controlled active suspension is ultimately a function of the response characteristics of the closed-loop plant and controller. Faster response and more complex controllers allow better disturbance rejection, but ultimately, the capabilities of an active suspension using feedback control are limited by the response characteristics of actuators and sensors [2], [3].
Instead of disturbance rejection using feedback control, preview control may be used. Preview information can allow for better disturbance rejection by allowing the controller to anticipate future events [2]–[4]. There are a variety of vision- and non-vision-based approaches that allow one to obtain an estimate of the road profile ahead of the vehicle [2], [5], [6]. The preview information can be used to adjust the suspension dynamics in order to minimize the effect of the upcoming disturbance. In effect, road preview information allows the system to shift from a feedback approach to a feedforward approach, in which the system is able to directly account for the disturbance [2], [4]. Ideally, the use of preview information would minimize the accelerations of the vehicle body, thus improving both ride comfort and safety [2], [4].
In practice, the application of preview information is not trivial, and several considerations have to be made before such an approach can be applied. First off, a road-preview suspension requires accurate preview information ahead of the vehicle [3], [5], [6]. Additionally, the control strategy utilized by the preview suspension must determine the necessary adjustments to the suspension based on the preview information [2], [3]. Finally, the actuation technology of the preview suspension must be capable of making the required adjustments in a timely manner [1], [3]. Additional challenges include environmental conditions, sensor noise, computational complexity, and the overall cost-effectiveness of the preview-based solution [1], [2], [6].
The purpose of this paper is to review the extent to which road-preview-based suspension approaches can improve the performance of an active suspension beyond what is possible with conventional feedback approaches. The paper will review the literature related to road-preview suspension approaches, including the identification of road-elevation profiles, preview-based control strategies, and control approaches related to feedforward control and model predictive control. Particular attention will be paid to improvements in ride comfort and vehicle-body acceleration, as well as the limitations imposed by sensing and computational considerations. Through this analysis, the paper will attempt to determine whether such approaches provide enough benefits to justify the additional complexities and costs they impose on the suspension system. These costs may be particularly significant in the context of emerging electrified, connected, and automated vehicles.

## II. Literature Review

Research about suspension systems is ongoing, and has been for many years; recently, there has been a growing interest in potentially using road preview data to enhance these systems. Studies in this area generally are divided into two separate parts: dealing with controlling the suspension, including tactics like feedback, feedforward, and predictive control; and estimating the road ahead using various sensors. Both parts are crucial, and even the best controllers could fail if the road data they receive isn’t precise or on time.
Yu et al. gave an overview of active suspension systems recently in their paper, “Advances in Active Suspension Systems for Road Vehicles” [1]. Both the hardware and the control strategies, including systems that are already used in production cars, are considered. They claim that active suspension is getting more attention in recent years because people want enhanced ride comfort as well as higher safety; electric and self-driving cars are also pushing this forward [1]. Another thing is that they point out how a good active suspension needs to be more than just theoretically sound. Real-world factors—how the actuators respond, how much power they use, how much additional weight they add, how reliable they are, and finally cost—all matter. This is important to note about road-preview systems, since adding better or more sensors and smarter control can improve performance. Indeed, it can also make the system more complex.
Theunissen et al. honed in on control methods that use road preview [2]. Specifically, they studied distinct preview-based control methods for semi-active and fully active suspensions. One key finding from them was that having road-preview information helps; it can make the suspension work better than controllers that don’t have it [2]. The benefits are limited, though; how much the preview helps depends entirely on the control method used, driving speed, road type, and how fast the actuator responds. Their review displayed that once sufficient preview information is available, inputting additional data doesn’t always help. They also talk about errors and noise; the quality of the road estimate, and therefore the data collected, matters a lot. Road preview is not only about seeing farther ahead, but rather getting information that is viable, fast, and actually usable.
Göhrle et al. looked at this problem much more directly. They developed methods to estimate the road profile, then control suspension using that information for a low bandwidth active suspension system [3]. Their work is significant, as it looks at both getting the preview signal and deciding how to use it. They developed two control methods: the first used feedforward compensation that was based on the preview signal together with a feedback controller; the second utilized Model Predictive Control (MPC) [3]. They also derived methods to combine road-height sensor readings over time, ignore noise, and correct for sensor instability. They tested their system with simulations and different vehicle measurements [3]. This study shows us that road-preview control is possible though tedious; it also shines light on just how closely connected road estimation and suspension control are.
Kaldas et al. looked at how adding road-preview information might help a suspension controller [4]. Their method utilized feedforward input from the road, along with feedback signals of the vehicle's state [4]. Their vehicle model had thirteen degrees of freedom and eight PID controllers [4]. The controller settings and preview distance were chosen to maximize ride comfort and road holding. They then tested the system with a measured road profile. Their results showed that the version with road preview technology could moderately improve ride performance (when compared with the same control approach without preview) [4]. This study supports the fundamental idea behind preview control: identifying a disturbance on the road the vehicle’s wheels get there can ensure the controller responds more effectively.
While control is certainly important, getting viable road intel is a major part of the challenge. Ni et al. looked at estimating the road profile with LiDAR sensors, along with GPS and inertial data [5]. In their system, various three-dimensional laser scanners were used to collect point-cloud data about the ground’s bumpiness; meanwhile, GPS and IMU data helped to determine the vehicle's orientation and positioning. This data was then put together into a precise elevation map, and then a Kalman filter was used to combine measurements into an even smoother estimate [5]. Finally, they hooked their road-estimation system up to an MPC-controlled suspension. Outdoor tests showed that the system could obtain road-elevation data, while the suspension helped maintain vehicle stability on uneven terrain [5]. It’s important to note, however, that they observed problems, like unreliable GPS information in some conditions, as well as the high computational requirements of MPC [5]. These issues highlight why a system that works so cleanly in testing can still be difficult to use in a production vehicle.
LiDAR is not the only way to collect road data. Lee and Yoon attempted to use stereo vision to estimate the road surface [6]. Cameras are a significantly lower-cost alternative to high end LiDAR; however, real driving introduces problems like noisier data, errors in the angle between the camera and the road, and interference from surrounding objects [6]. Their methods first identify the road area, then build a digital elevation map, and finally create the road-surface profile. The elevation map is constantly updated over time, using filtering to eliminate noise and keep measurements consistent [6]. They used driving image sequences to put their method to the test, and their results showed reductions in elevation noise as well as pitch-angle errors compared with other methods that did not use the same level of temporal consistency [6]. Nonetheless, this work is useful, showcasing to others both the potential benefits and drawbacks of using simple cameras rather than more expensive laser-based sensing.
Looking at all these studies collectively, the main idea presents itself: road-preview information plays a huge role in improving suspension systems, but is very challenging to actually implement. Theunissen et al., Göhrle et al. and Kaldas et al. all show that detecting and locating road disturbances before they can reach the wheels improves control performance [2]–[4]. Ni et al. and Lee and Yoon show two potential ways of getting that information; they also run into and bring to light problems such as precise measurement errors and higher computational demands [5], [6]. Yu et al. put this into a larger context by asserting that a system must perform well while also being practical enough for real vehicles [1].
A big pattern across all of these studies is that the entire system matters. Better sensors are only useful if the controller can implement the data fast enough; a smart controller is limited if the actuator cannot react efficiently. Due to this, the real question is not whether road preview can or can not improve performance; the more important issue is how much improvement it provides once the limits of sensing, computing, actuation, and cost are fully considered. This balance between performance and practicality is the next subject in this paper.

## III. Road-Preview Sensing and Control

### A. Road-Preview Sensing

Road-preview sensing grants vehicles the ability to evaluate the road ahead for unevenness before the wheels get there. Cameras, LiDAR, and other sensors on the vehicle are all common approaches to graph out bumpiness before it’s felt. Cameras can get road-surface intel by processing images and analyzing patterns for depth, while three-dimensional sensing like LiDar provides more accurate measurements of road geometry. Sensor fusion is combining data from several different sensors with vehicle motion data to collect information and improve road estimation [2],[5],[6].
Obviously, the accuracy of the estimated road profile is crucial to how good the preview-based suspension control will be. Errors due to sensor noise, poor environmental conditions, vehicle motion, and limitations in generally reconstructing the road visually using sensors can reduce the reliability of the preview’s information. Furthermore, there’s always going to be a delay when sensing and processing the road, reducing the time available for actuators to respond before the wheel passes the sensed area. Vehicles are designed to cover great distances in little time, meaning that sensors must be long ranged, accurate, and quick to be viable and worth it compared to a passive suspension system [2],[5],[6].

### B. Preview-Based Control Strategies

When the road profile ahead is evaluated, the information can be fed to the suspension controller. Feedforward control is meant to take both previous and recent data on the anticipated disturbance and calculate a suspension response before it affects the vehicle. When it’s combined with conventional feedback control, the two working together can anticipate road irregularities and correct deviations in vehicle motion at the same time [2],[4]. 

MPC, or Model Predictive Control, is another valid approach; using a mathematical model of the vehicle, it predicts the future behavior of it, and determines suitable control actions over a defined prediction horizon. When road-preview data is also available, the controller can both account for future disturbances and select suspension to balance ride comfort, suspension travel, and road holding. The effectiveness of MPC is dependent, however, on the accuracy of the vehicle model, the depth of the road-preview data, and how fast and accurately that data can be computed [2],[3].

Overall, road-preview sensing with the implementation of feedforward control or MPC grants valuable intel that passive, feedback-only systems don't usually have: estimates of the road bumpiness before it reaches the vehicle. Taking this information and combining it with some form of road-preview sensing minimizes the impact of road disturbances on ride comfort. It is important to note that the resulting benefits do depend on sensing accuracy, actuator capabilities, and processing delays [2],[3],[4].


## IV. Performance Benefits and Limitations

### A. Performance Benefits

Road-preview sensing offers many potential advantages over more traditional feedback suspension control, especially in ride comfort and eliminating disturbances. By estimating asymmetry in the road ahead, the controller can deploy suspension adjustments as needed before impact. Theunissen et al. compared different preview-based control strategies, and concluded that effectiveness was primarily dependent on controller design, actuator responsiveness, and sensed information. 
Kaldas et. al. showed the potential benefits of combining road-preview information with conventional feedback and feedforward control. They found better ride performance in active suspension when compared to control approaches without preview intel [4]. Göhrle et al. looked into road-profile estimation with feedforward and Model Predictive control strategies, showing the value of implementing road estimation alongside suspension control [3]. These studies both indicate that preview information improves suspension performance, allowing the controller to predict bumpiness rather than relying solely on on-the-spot, reactive corrections.
The magnitude of these changes, however, varies with several factors. Indeed, the control architecture, road conditions, and performance measures evaluated all play a factor in that. Reducing vehicle acceleration doesn’t always mean an improvement in it’s dynamics. Effective control needs to balance ride comfort, road handling, actuator constraints, and suspension effectiveness all at once [1],[2]. 

### B. Technical and Practical Limitations

While it may have many potential benefits, road-preview suspension introduces many technical challenges. Primarily, the controller depends on proper estimation and sensing. Sensor noise, environmental abnormalities, sudden vehicle motion, and errors in digital road reconstruction all make preview information less trustable. Ni et al. explored LiDAR-based road-profile estimation, identifying challenges connected to GPS reliability as well as the computational demands of predictive control [5]. Similarly, Lee and Yoon demonstrated how crucial reducing measurement noise and maintaining unvarying road-surface estimates when using stereo vision [6]. 

Secondly, the suspension must computate preview information and react quickly enough to make adjustments right as they’re needed to handle the disturbance. Therefore, active suspension systems can be affected by computational delays, limited preview time, and actuator bandwidth [2],[3]. More advanced controllers, like MPC, require additional computational firepower to properly estimate vehicle behavior, and calculate appropriate control actions [3],[5]. 

The additional processing hardware, sensors, control components, and calibration requirements necessary increase cost, and usually system complexity. Yu et al. emphasized that to be realistic for commercial purposes, active suspension systems need to account for not only energy consumption and performance, but affordability and long-term reliability. To be cost effective and factory friendly, theoretical performance improvements need to be taken at a realistic face value [1]. 
In conclusion, road-preview sensing improves action suspension performance in many cases, yet practical value is dependent on how major the improvements are, specifically if they’re warranted when considering engineering complexity and cost. Competent sensing, computation, and actuator capability are essential for translating potential theoretical benefits researched and modeled into reliable, practical effectuation. 

## V. Conclusion

Road-preview sensing is a new and exciting approach to active vehicle suspension, allowing the system to discover road unevenness before the vehicle body gets to them. As discussed in the paper, technologies like LiDAR and stereo vision help to estimate road conditions prior to contact, and can improve ride comfort and vehicle response better than systems with just feedback control.

The results are based on more than just having information on the road ahead, however. Precise sensors, fast processing, controller performance and actuator limitations all affect the system’s effectiveness. More hardware and cost makes implementation harder, especially in commercial vehicles. While research demonstrates that there are promising opportunities for implementation, applying theoretical active suspension concepts into the real world means accounting for these challenges.
Overall, road-preview sensing may improve active suspension in the future, but it’s an incomplete solution by itself. Implementing it successfully depends on the compatibility and competence of its control components, and a careful evaluation on if the performance benefits make the additional build cost and complexity worth it. In the future, better road estimation and real-time control could make these intricate systems more widely implemented.

